<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter6_arrays-of-structures-and-objects">
    <title>Arrays of Structures and Objects</title>

    <p>
        An array can store objects created from a structure or class. This lets
        a program process several related objects with the same loop pattern.
    </p>

    <p>
        The following example uses the <c>Point</c> structure from Chapter 5.
        The range-based <c>for</c> loop visits each point and accesses its public
        data members.
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_array_of_points"
             language="cpp"
             interactive="yes">
        <code><![CDATA[
#include <array>
#include <iostream>

struct Point {
    double x;
    double y;
};

int main() {
    const std::array<Point, 3> points{
        Point{1.0, 2.0},
        Point{3.0, 4.0},
        Point{5.0, 6.0}
    };

    for (const Point& point : points) {
        std::cout << '(' << point.x << ", " << point.y << ")\n";
    }

    return 0;
}
        ]]></code>
    </program>

    <p>
        The declaration <c>const Point&amp; point</c> gives the loop a read-only
        reference to each object. The loop does not need to copy the point, and
        it cannot modify the array's objects through that reference.
    </p>

    <p>
        The same idea works with class objects. Instead of accessing public data,
        the loop can call a public member function on each object.
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_array_of_rectangles"
             language="cpp"
             interactive="yes">
        <code><![CDATA[
#include <array>
#include <iostream>

class Rectangle {
private:
    double width;
    double height;

public:
    Rectangle(double rectangle_width, double rectangle_height)
        : width{rectangle_width}, height{rectangle_height} {
    }

    double calculate_area() const {
        return width * height;
    }
};

int main() {
    const std::array<Rectangle, 2> rectangles{
        Rectangle{4.0, 3.0},
        Rectangle{5.0, 2.0}
    };

    for (const Rectangle& rectangle : rectangles) {
        std::cout << rectangle.calculate_area() << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
        ]]></code>
    </program>

    <p>
        The phrase <q>for each object</q> is a useful clue that a range-based
        loop may be appropriate. The type of the object determines whether the
        loop accesses data members or calls member functions.
    </p>
</section>
